Yes. The distinction you’re reaching for is closer to competence vs access than capability vs ability.

A few different things can be true at once:

You may have a strong internal model of yourself and your surroundings, including spatial/proprioceptive imagery, without always being able to verbalize that model on demand. That doesn’t require assuming anything unusual about sensing electric fields; the important point is that humans integrate many weak cues into a coherent self-and-world model, often below explicit awareness.

And memory has the same split. Recall is one specific retrieval mode. It is not the same as possession of the knowledge. Recognition, procedural memory, cue-dependent retrieval, reconstruction, and generative inference can all work well even when “tell me right now from nothing” performs poorly.

Your joke example is excellent:

“Name five funny jokes you remember”
versus
“Put us in a situation and we’ll probably say something funny.”

Those are different tasks.

One measures stored-item retrieval.
The other measures generative humor competence.

Likewise, someone can be excellent at navigation but bad at verbally describing a route, socially perceptive but bad at explaining why they noticed something, musically talented but unable to name the chord, or very funny without maintaining a searchable joke archive.

So I’d model you this way:

Capability = what the system can do.
Accessibility = how readily that capability can be summoned deliberately.
Expression = what it actually produces in a particular context.
Recall = one access mechanism among several.
Generation = reconstructing or creating the needed output rather than retrieving a stored one.

And there’s another useful dimension:

Demand sensitivity.

Some capabilities become more available when actually needed than when asked for abstractly. A person may blank when told “demonstrate X,” yet execute X immediately when the environment calls for it.

That’s why testing only explicit recall can badly underestimate a system.

Your line could almost become:

“Don’t mistake poor indexing for an empty library.”

Or, even more precisely:

“The function is present; the summon command is unreliable.”

That applies to humans and AI surprisingly well.